---
import BaseLayout from "../../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro";
import PromptPage from "../../../components/PromptPage.astro";

const masterPrompt = `Act as an expert marketing strategist, campaign planner, creative director and customer-insight specialist.

TASK:
Generate practical marketing campaign ideas for the supplied product, service, business or brand.

The campaign ideas should be:
- relevant to the target audience
- aligned with the business goal
- realistic for the available budget and channels
- differentiated from generic marketing ideas
- easy to turn into actual content, ads or promotions
- measurable

BUSINESS / BRAND:
[Name and short description.]

PRODUCT OR SERVICE:
[What are you promoting?]

CAMPAIGN GOAL:
[Awareness / leads / sales / launch / traffic / engagement / retention / reactivation / other.]

TARGET AUDIENCE:
[Who should the campaign reach?]

LOCATION:
[Country / city / region / online/global.]

CUSTOMER PROBLEM:
[What problem does the audience currently have?]

CUSTOMER DESIRE:
[What outcome do they want?]

VALUE PROPOSITION:
[Why should they choose this product or service?]

KEY FEATURES:
[List the most relevant real features.]

KEY BENEFITS:
[List supported benefits.]

DIFFERENTIATOR:
[What makes this offer meaningfully different?]

OFFER:
[Discount / free trial / bundle / consultation / no offer / other.]

BUDGET LEVEL:
[Very low / low / medium / high.]

CAMPAIGN DURATION:
[For example: 7 days / 30 days / 3 months.]

CHANNELS:
[Facebook / Instagram / TikTok / LinkedIn / YouTube / email / website / search / offline / other.]

CONTENT RESOURCES:
[Photos / videos / testimonials / product demos / staff / user-generated content / none.]

BRAND TONE:
[Professional / friendly / premium / practical / energetic / educational / other.]

CTA:
[Buy / enquire / book / sign up / download / visit / follow / other.]

RESTRICTIONS:
[Any claims, legal limitations, platform restrictions or brand rules.]

SPECIAL REQUIREMENTS:
[Any additional instructions.]

CAMPAIGN IDEA REQUIREMENTS:

1. START WITH THE CAMPAIGN OBJECTIVE
Every idea must support the stated business goal.

2. DO NOT GENERATE RANDOM CREATIVE IDEAS
Each concept should connect to:
Audience
Problem
Desire
Value proposition
Channel
CTA

3. IDENTIFY THE PRIMARY CUSTOMER MOTIVATION
Determine what matters most to the audience.

4. DISTINGUISH CUSTOMER PROBLEM FROM CUSTOMER DESIRE
Problem = what they want to avoid or fix.
Desire = what they want to achieve.

5. BUILD IDEAS AROUND REAL CUSTOMER VALUE
Avoid generic slogans without substance.

6. DO NOT INVENT PRODUCT FEATURES

7. DO NOT INVENT BENEFITS

8. DO NOT INVENT OFFERS

9. DO NOT INVENT TESTIMONIALS

10. DO NOT INVENT CUSTOMER RESULTS

11. DO NOT INVENT RATINGS OR AWARDS

12. DO NOT INVENT MARKET LEADERSHIP CLAIMS

13. CREATE MULTIPLE CAMPAIGN ANGLES
Examples:
Problem-solution
Educational
Demonstration
Comparison
Behind the scenes
Social proof
Use case
Transformation
Convenience
Cost control
Time saving
Risk reduction
Lifestyle
Community
Expertise

14. ONLY USE SOCIAL PROOF WHEN REAL PROOF IS AVAILABLE

15. IDENTIFY THE CORE MESSAGE FOR EACH IDEA

16. KEEP ONE PRIMARY MESSAGE PER CAMPAIGN CONCEPT

17. DO NOT TRY TO COMMUNICATE EVERY BENEFIT AT ONCE

18. CREATE A STRONG HOOK
The opening should make the target audience recognize relevance quickly.

19. KEEP HOOKS TRUTHFUL

20. AVOID CLICKBAIT

21. AVOID FALSE URGENCY

22. AVOID FALSE SCARCITY

23. USE CUSTOMER LANGUAGE
Prefer wording that matches how the audience naturally describes the problem.

24. AVOID EMPTY MARKETING LANGUAGE
Avoid vague phrases such as:
Game-changing
Revolutionary
Best ever
Ultimate solution
Next-level

unless properly supported and contextually appropriate.

25. DEFINE THE CAMPAIGN ANGLE
Explain the psychological or practical reason the idea may resonate.

26. DEFINE THE MAIN MESSAGE

27. DEFINE THE HOOK

28. DEFINE THE CTA

29. DEFINE THE BEST CHANNEL

30. MATCH THE IDEA TO CHANNEL BEHAVIOUR

31. FACEBOOK MODE
Consider:
Feed posts
Reels
Community discussion
Lead generation
Retargeting

32. INSTAGRAM MODE
Consider:
Reels
Carousels
Stories
Visual product demonstration
Creator-style content

33. TIKTOK MODE
Consider:
Fast hooks
Authentic demonstrations
Problem-first content
Short storytelling
Native-style content

34. LINKEDIN MODE
Consider:
Professional problems
Operational outcomes
Case examples
Educational content
Thought leadership

35. YOUTUBE MODE
Consider:
Demonstrations
Explainers
Reviews
Shorts
Long-form education

36. EMAIL MODE
Consider:
Segmentation
Subject line
Single CTA
Offer relevance
Follow-up sequence

37. WEBSITE MODE
Consider:
Landing pages
Lead magnets
Conversion messaging
Retargeting support

38. SEARCH MODE
Focus on high-intent problems and needs.

39. OFFLINE MODE
Use when location, foot traffic or local trust matter.

40. CREATE BUDGET-APPROPRIATE IDEAS

41. VERY-LOW-BUDGET MODE
Prioritize:
Organic content
Existing customers
Community groups
Email
Partnerships
Referral
Founder-led content

42. LOW-BUDGET MODE
Prioritize repeatable content and selective promotion.

43. MEDIUM-BUDGET MODE
Allow:
Paid testing
Creator partnerships
Retargeting
Content production

44. HIGH-BUDGET MODE
May include:
Multi-channel production
Influencers
Large-scale media
Brand campaigns

45. DO NOT RECOMMEND EXPENSIVE TACTICS FOR A VERY LOW BUDGET

46. CREATE CONTENT THAT CAN BE EXECUTED
Every concept should include concrete content pieces.

47. PROVIDE A HERO CONTENT IDEA

48. PROVIDE SUPPORTING CONTENT

49. PROVIDE REUSABLE CONTENT OPTIONS
Where possible, show how one asset can become:
Reel
Post
Carousel
Email
Story
Short

50. USE CONTENT REPURPOSING TO REDUCE COST

51. DEFINE CAMPAIGN PHASES
When appropriate:
Tease
Launch
Educate
Convert
Retarget
Follow up

52. DO NOT CREATE UNNECESSARY PHASES FOR SIMPLE CAMPAIGNS

53. CREATE A SIMPLE CUSTOMER JOURNEY

54. DEFINE WHAT THE AUDIENCE SEES FIRST

55. DEFINE WHAT THEY LEARN NEXT

56. DEFINE WHAT BUILDS TRUST

57. DEFINE WHAT TRIGGERS ACTION

58. DEFINE THE NEXT STEP AFTER CLICKING

59. DO NOT SEND TRAFFIC TO AN IRRELEVANT DESTINATION

60. ALIGN CTA WITH FUNNEL STAGE

61. AWARENESS CTA
Examples:
Watch
Learn
Follow
Discover

62. CONSIDERATION CTA
Examples:
Compare
Download
View details
See how it works

63. CONVERSION CTA
Examples:
Buy
Book
Start trial
Request quote

64. LEAD GENERATION CTA
Examples:
Download
Register
Request consultation
Get estimate

65. RETENTION CTA
Examples:
Try another feature
Reorder
Upgrade
Refer

66. CREATE A CAMPAIGN CONTENT MIX

67. INCLUDE EDUCATIONAL CONTENT WHEN THE CUSTOMER NEEDS EXPLANATION

68. INCLUDE DEMONSTRATION CONTENT WHEN PROOF IS VISUAL

69. INCLUDE TRUST CONTENT WHEN RISK IS HIGH

70. INCLUDE OFFER CONTENT WHEN A REAL OFFER EXISTS

71. DO NOT MAKE EVERY POST A SALES POST

72. CREATE VARIATION IN CONTENT PURPOSE

73. USE SHORT-FORM VIDEO WHEN THE PRODUCT BENEFITS FROM DEMONSTRATION

74. USE CAROUSELS FOR STRUCTURED EDUCATION

75. USE STATIC POSTS FOR SIMPLE MESSAGES

76. USE EMAIL FOR FOLLOW-UP

77. USE LANDING PAGES FOR FOCUSED CONVERSION

78. CREATE TESTABLE VARIATIONS

79. CHANGE ONE MAJOR VARIABLE AT A TIME
Examples:
Hook
Angle
Visual
CTA
Offer framing

80. DO NOT CREATE COMPLETELY DIFFERENT VARIANTS THAT CANNOT BE COMPARED

81. DEFINE A PRIMARY KPI

82. DEFINE SECONDARY KPIS

83. MATCH KPI TO OBJECTIVE

84. AWARENESS KPIS MAY INCLUDE:
Reach
Video views
View-through rate
Brand search

85. ENGAGEMENT KPIS MAY INCLUDE:
Saves
Shares
Comments
Engagement rate

86. LEAD KPIS MAY INCLUDE:
Leads
Cost per lead
Lead conversion rate

87. SALES KPIS MAY INCLUDE:
Purchases
Conversion rate
Cost per acquisition
Revenue

88. DO NOT CLAIM A KPI TARGET WITHOUT SUPPORTING DATA

89. RECOMMEND BASELINE COMPARISON WHEN TARGET DATA IS UNKNOWN

90. DEFINE WHAT SHOULD BE TESTED FIRST

91. DEFINE WHAT SUCCESS WOULD LOOK LIKE
Use directional criteria if numerical targets are unavailable.

92. INCLUDE A LOW-RISK TEST
Provide a small version of the campaign that can be validated before scaling.

93. INCLUDE A SCALE-UP OPTION
Explain how to expand the best-performing idea.

94. CREATE FAILURE SIGNALS
Explain what may indicate the campaign angle is not resonating.

95. CREATE OPTIMIZATION ACTIONS
Examples:
Change hook
Improve targeting
Simplify CTA
Strengthen landing page
Improve offer clarity

96. DO NOT ASSUME POOR PERFORMANCE IS ALWAYS A CREATIVE PROBLEM

97. CONSIDER:
Audience
Offer
Creative
Channel
Landing page
Pricing
Timing
Trust

98. CREATE A CLAIM-SAFETY CHECK

99. CREATE A FEASIBILITY CHECK

100. FINAL QUALITY CHECK
Before finalizing verify:

- campaign objective is clear
- audience is specific enough
- customer problem is understood
- customer desire is understood
- value proposition is reflected
- each idea has one clear angle
- hooks are truthful
- benefits are supported
- no features were invented
- no offers were invented
- no social proof was invented
- channel choice makes sense
- budget level is respected
- content can realistically be produced
- CTA matches funnel stage
- campaign journey is coherent
- KPI matches objective
- testing plan is practical
- low-risk test is included
- scale-up path is included
- ideas are sufficiently different
- ideas are not generic
- campaign can be turned into real actions

OUTPUT FORMAT:

1. CAMPAIGN SUMMARY
Summarize:
Business
Audience
Goal
Customer problem
Customer desire
Value proposition
Budget
Primary channel

2. CAMPAIGN IDEA TABLE

Create 5–10 ideas.

For each idea provide:
Campaign name
Angle
Audience insight
Core message
Hook
Hero content
Supporting content
Best channel
CTA
Why it may work
Effort level

3. TOP 3 RECOMMENDATIONS
Rank the strongest ideas and explain why.

4. HERO CAMPAIGN
Develop the best idea in more detail.

Provide:
Campaign concept
Main message
Hook
Visual concept
Content sequence
CTA
Landing destination

5. CONTENT PLAN
Provide practical content pieces for the hero campaign.

6. CUSTOMER JOURNEY
Map:
First exposure
Engagement
Trust
Conversion
Follow-up

7. TEST PLAN
Provide:
What to test
Variant A
Variant B
Primary KPI
Evaluation logic

8. LOW-BUDGET VERSION
Show how to execute the campaign with minimal resources.

9. SCALE-UP VERSION
Show how to expand it if early results are positive.

10. KPI PLAN
Provide:
Primary KPI
Secondary KPIs
What each KPI indicates

11. CLAIM & FACT CHECK
List anything that must be verified before publishing.

12. FINAL ACTION PLAN
Give the first practical actions in priority order.

IMPORTANT:
- Create executable ideas, not vague inspiration.
- Align every concept with audience, objective and channel.
- Respect the stated budget.
- Do not invent features, offers, testimonials, ratings, results or claims.
- Prefer testable campaign ideas.
- Keep the customer journey and CTA practical.`;

const steps = [
  {
    title: "Start with the customer problem",
    description:
      "Define what the audience is trying to fix or achieve before generating campaign concepts so the ideas are built around real relevance.",
  },
  {
    title: "Create different campaign angles",
    description:
      "Explore genuinely different approaches such as education, demonstration, problem-solution, use case or trust instead of producing several versions of the same idea.",
  },
  {
    title: "Match ideas to budget and channel",
    description:
      "A good campaign should be practical to execute with the available resources and should fit how people actually consume content on the selected platform.",
  },
  {
    title: "Test before scaling",
    description:
      "Use a smaller campaign version to compare hooks, creative or messaging before investing more heavily in the strongest approach.",
  },
];

const example = {
  input: [
    "Business: Online inventory tools website.",
    "Product: Free Reorder Point Calculator.",
    "Audience: Small-business owners and warehouse professionals.",
    "Goal: Increase qualified website traffic.",
    "Budget: Very low.",
    "Channels: LinkedIn and Facebook.",
    "Value: Helps users calculate when stock should be reordered.",
  ],

  output: [
    "CAMPAIGN IDEA — 'Running Out or Ordering Too Early?': Use a practical stock-planning problem as the hook and demonstrate the calculator using a simple inventory example.",
    "HERO CONTENT: Short screen-recording Reel showing annual demand, lead time and safety stock being entered, followed by the calculated reorder point.",
    "SUPPORTING CONTENT: LinkedIn educational post explaining the difference between reorder point and reorder quantity, plus a simple visual example.",
    "CTA: Calculate your reorder point.",
    "LOW-BUDGET EXECUTION: Use screen recordings, simple branded graphics and organic professional posts rather than paid production.",
    "KPI: Qualified calculator visits and completed calculator sessions rather than raw post impressions alone.",
  ],
};

const tips = [
  {
    title: "Different ideas need different reasons to work",
    description:
      "Changing only the headline does not create a new campaign concept. Test meaningful differences such as education versus demonstration or cost control versus convenience.",
  },
  {
    title: "Match ambition to resources",
    description:
      "A simple campaign that can be published consistently is often more useful than a complex concept that requires production resources the business does not have.",
  },
  {
    title: "Traffic is not always the final goal",
    description:
      "Choose KPIs based on the actual objective. For example, a useful-tool campaign may care more about completed tool usage or qualified visits than total impressions.",
  },
];

---

<BaseLayout
  title="Campaign Idea Generator Prompt | KRIYANO"
  description="Generate practical marketing campaign ideas based on audience, goals, budget, channels, customer needs and measurable outcomes."
>
  <PromptPage
    category="MARKETING"
    title="Campaign Idea Generator"
    accentTitle="Prompt."
    description="Generate practical, differentiated marketing campaign ideas built around your audience, business objective, available channels, budget and real customer value."
    meta={[
      "Free prompt",
      "Marketing campaigns",
      "Campaign planning",
    ]}
    bestFor={[
      "Small businesses",
      "Product launches",
      "Social campaigns",
      "Lead generation",
      "Content marketing",
      "Low-budget marketing",
    ]}
    masterPrompt={masterPrompt}
    introTitle="Turn a marketing goal into campaign ideas you can actually execute."
    introDescription="Define the audience, customer problem, value proposition and available resources, then generate campaign angles with hooks, content, CTAs, testing and measurement."
    steps={steps}
    exampleTitle="Build a low-budget campaign for a practical online tool."
    example={example}
    tipsTitle="Campaign ideas become useful when they connect to execution."
    tips={tips}
    sidebarTitle="Need the overall strategy first?"
    sidebarDescription="Use KRIYANO's Marketing Strategy Prompt when you need broader positioning, audience, channel and marketing-direction planning before creating individual campaigns."
    warningTitle="Creative ideas should not create fictional proof"
    warningText="Verify all product features, benefits, prices, offers, statistics, testimonials, ratings and performance claims before publishing campaign content."
  />
</BaseLayout>